<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gospel of John</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 90–110 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gospel of John is the fourth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">canonical gospels</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>, probably written around 90–110 CE. Its account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> differs from the other three gospels, with long discourses and a high Christology that opens with the Word (Logos) made flesh. Early Christian tradition linked it to the apostle John and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, where he was said to have lived in old age.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john.jpg" alt="Gospel of John" width="320" height="433" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RylandsImaging · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JRL19060215_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The gospel is internally anonymous. It identifies an unnamed 'disciple whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> loved' as its source and, perhaps, its author, a figure who appears at key episodes such as the Last Supper and the Empty Tomb. The traditional attribution to John the Apostle is first found in Irenaeus. Many modern scholars still affirm it, but most prefer alternatives such as John the Elder or Lazarus, while holding that the beloved disciple is the source of much of the content. Most critical commentaries consider that it was probably composed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The gospel is usually divided into a prologue (1:1 to 1:18), an account of the ministry often called the Book of Signs (1:19 to 12:50), the final night, passion and resurrection (13:1 to 20:31) and an epilogue in chapter 21. The first part presents seven signs that culminate in the raising of Lazarus, and the second the Farewell Discourse and the passion and resurrection. The prologue identifies <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> as the Logos, the Word, and calls the Word God; it contains some of the clearest identifications of Jesus with God in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>.</p><p>It differs from the Synoptic Gospels. The ministry lasts three years, marked by three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passovers</a>, and the cleansing of the Temple comes at its start. The death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> is presented as his glorification and return to the Father, and he speaks of being lifted up. In place of the institution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a>, Jesus washes his disciples' feet. About 85 percent of its content is unique to it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>There is evidence that the gospel was circulating by the early second century: the First Epistle of John, which depends on it, was cited by Papias of Hierapolis. The attribution to John son of Zebedee was widespread by the mid-second century. Since the 19th century scholars have agreed that its long discourses are less likely to be historical than the Synoptic parables, though it is not without historical value, for example in its topographical references to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and Judea. Early in the 20th century many scholars saw Gnostic elements in it, but most now consider the question closed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine of Hippo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a> wrote commentaries on it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gospel of John” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its later greatness as the leading city of Roman Asia, a centre of early Christianity linked with the apostle Paul and the <b>Gospel of John</b>, and a council city of the church in 431 CE, rested on the foundations laid in these centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>He called himself an apostle of Jesus Christ and the Paraclete, the helper promised in the <b>Gospel of John</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/manichaeism">Manichaeism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The only lengthy text known to have been composed in Gothic is the Skeireins, a few pages of commentary on the <b>Gospel of John</b>.</blockquote><a href="/language/gothic">Gothic · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>…a convert to Christianity from the Essene community, because the odes resemble both the writings found at Qumran and the <b>Gospel of John</b>; James H. Charlesworth concludes that he was an Essene convert to the Johannine community, and some have argued that he had seen John the Baptist.</blockquote><a href="/songs/odes-of-solomon">Odes of Solomon</a></li><li><blockquote>Christians connect its shepherd with Jesus, called the Good Shepherd in the <b>Gospel of John</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/psalm-23">Psalm 23</a></li><li><blockquote>Others link its image of God stirring the water to the <b>Gospel of John</b>, chapter 5, where a sick man waits beside the pool of Bethesda to be healed when its water is troubled.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wade-in-the-water">Wade in the Water</a></li></ul></section>
